  "worked_solution": "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at the values of $x$ which make the denominator equal to zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero, we have $x-1=0,$ which corresponds to $x=1.$ \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-1,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,2]$ and $[-1,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,2]$ and $[0,2]$ contain the point $x=1.$",
